WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.100
- [Narrator] This is Chris.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.013
No, wait. That's not average enough.

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:06.720
(keyboard clicking)

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.540
James. Okay, this is James.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.820
He just graduated with a bachelor's degree

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.700
and $30,000 in student loan debt,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.350
which is a rounded average amount

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.930
of what most graduates
in the US end up with,

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.480
on which he pays a 5% interest rate,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.910
which is again pretty average.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.090
And this is Mary.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.300
Mary also took out the exact same loan.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.410
James's payment plan

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.380
allowed him to be debt free in 10 years

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:35.880
while Mary's repayment plan

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.700
meant that she ended up owing more money

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.700
after paying towards
the loan for a decade.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.530
The Supreme Court is about to decide

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.150
the fate of President Biden's plan

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.990
to forgive up to $20,000
in student debt, but.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.870
- That is by no means the only big thing

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.630
that the Biden administration
has done on student debt.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.640
One of his other moves
that was actually announced

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.860
the same day as mass debt cancellation

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.150
got much less attention,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.430
but is arguably gonna make
a much bigger difference

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.220
in the long run for
student loan borrowers.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.080
- [Narrator] The other
big part of Biden's plan

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.790
will change how Mary, James,

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.970
and most other current
and future borrowers

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.980
will repay their student loans.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.010
Here's how. Let's start with James.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:22.010
On this loan, his monthly
payment would be $318,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.270
and this is his payment schedule.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.980
It's like a mortgage or car loan

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.100
where the interest essentially
gets paid off first.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.900
So his first month, James
owes $125 in interest.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.290
So the remaining $193
of his monthly payment

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.530
goes towards the loan's
original amount, the principle,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.140
which then lowers his total balance.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.810
It's called amortization.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.820
Each month, less of his monthly payment

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.060
goes towards interest and
more towards the principle.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.030
Those monthly payments make
his total amount paid go up

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.930
and his balance owed go down
until at the end of 10 years

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.370
he's paid off the loan
paying $38,000 in total.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.560
James's experience is
what repayment looks like

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.130
for more than 40% of all
student loan borrowers.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.230
While he can afford those monthly payments

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.960
on his $60,000 a year salary,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.890
that's too much for Mary
who makes $30,000 a year.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.920
So she did what nearly 40% of borrowers do

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.650
and went onto an
income-based repayment plan

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.000
which lowered her monthly
payment to around $68 a month.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.770
But with amortization,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.580
she still owes that $125 the first month

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.040
since it's the same loan as James,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.410
but Mary's monthly
payment is less than that,

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.650
meaning there's nothing to go
towards the loan's principle

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.710
and her balance owed goes up.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.860
Her monthly payments only
go towards the interest,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.350
which makes her total amount paid go up,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.840
but also her balance.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.220
At the end of 10 years,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.760
Mary has paid $8,000
towards her student loans,

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.020
but she still owes more than $38,000.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.510
This situation is a common one.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.520
The exact number of Marys is unknown,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.580
but a Pew survey found
that two in five borrowers

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.040
owe more than they originally took out.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.110
- I hear from people every single day

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.000
whose current balances,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.100
even after they've been
paying off their student loans

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.200
for 5, 10, 15 years,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.350
are larger than the
balance they started with.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.180
- [Narrator] The income-driven
repayment programs

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.930
are set up to only last 20 or 25 years.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.910
So in theory, people like Mary

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.060
will have paid $16,000 after two decades

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.810
and her then $53,000
balance would be forgiven,

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:37.980
but a government report

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.950
found that of all the
borrowers at that stage,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.580
only 11% would be
eligible for forgiveness.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:45.840
- It shows how common it is

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.900
that so few people are able
to complete these programs.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.990
What's uncommon is successfully completing

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.600
the income-driven repayment
program as it was designed.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.950
That's what's uncommon.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.630
- [Narrator] If they miss one payment,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.060
they become unenrolled in the program.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.650
- They could have not submitted

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.230
their income recertification information,

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.690
which is necessary every single year,

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.090
or there could have been some data issue

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.840
where a payment to a servicer
did not get recorded properly

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.600
and as a result you are no longer eligible

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.370
to be part of this program.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.630
- [Narrator] If that happened to Mary,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.570
she would be on the hook
for her entire balance

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.000
with all that built up interest.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.370
Re-enrolling in the
program is her only path

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.780
to earn forgiveness.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.360
This is something President
Biden's student loan plan

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.860
attempts to fix.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.530
Under the new system,
any interest Mary accrued

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:37.980
while on the income-driven
repayment plan would be forgiven,

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:41.220
so her payments would go
directly to the loan's principle

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.440
instead of just interest.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.470
The new plan also changes the
rules around who qualifies

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.810
and lowers their monthly payments.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.360
Mary, with her $30,000 a year salary,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.700
would likely have no monthly payment.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.360
Even James would be eligible
for a smaller monthly payment.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.960
And dramatically, if
someone, let's say Robert,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.350
has $12,000 or less in
undergraduate loans,

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.940
they would have their debt forgiven

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.050
after 10 years of small or
sometimes zero dollar payments.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.010
- No one with an undergraduate
loan today or in the future,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.890
whether for community college
or a four year college,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:21.330
will have to pay more than 5%
of their discretionary income

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.340
to repay their loan.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.440
- So this is really
shifting the boundaries

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.500
between what counts as a loan
and what counts as a grant.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.030
This proposal probably goes farther

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.800
than President Biden intended to go,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.440
at least while he was
campaigning for president,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.540
but that largely reflects the reality

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.180
that nothing was getting done
in Congress on this issue

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.110
and he felt for political
or economic reasons

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.480
or other reasons that he
needed to move forward

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.370
on an executive action basis

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.400
or through using powers
that were available to him

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.690
under existing law.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.340
- [Narrator] Powers not everyone agrees

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.900
he should be able to use for this.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.040
- Because President Biden

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.380
couldn't get his radical
agenda through Congress,

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.620
he is dismantling the
Federal Student Loan Program

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.620
and pushing Democrat's free
college plan by executive fiat.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.780
- [Narrator] These changes
go into effect in July

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.490
regardless of how the Supreme Court

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.350
rolls on debt forgiveness.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.960
This plan is also going to be expensive.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.780
One report found that while
forgiveness would cost the US

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.410
nearly $500 billion
over the next 10 years,

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.420
the changes to income-driven repayment

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.720
would cost 141 billion or more

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.393
as more borrowers take
advantage of the program.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.300
While forgiveness is criticized

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.160
as solving a problem for the past

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.530
without creating a future solution,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.060
this plan looks to change the way

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.730
student loans are handled going forward

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:46.214
so Mary doesn't end up owing
$60,000 for her $30,000 degree.

00:06:46.214 --> 00:06:48.797
(bright music)

